   "source": [
    "# Residual Network(ResNet)\n",
    "\n",
    "**ResNet** (Residual Network) is a deep convolutional neural network architecture introduced by researchers from Microsoft Research in the paper:\n",
    "\n",
    "> [Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition](https://www.cv-foundation.org/openaccess/content_cvpr_2016/papers/He_Deep_Residual_Learning_CVPR_2016_paper.pdf)\n",
    "\n",
    "It won the ILSVRC 2015 competition and became one of the most influential architectures in computer vision.\n",
    "\n",
    "ResNet solved a major problem in deep neural networks:\n",
    "\n",
    "> As networks become very deep, training accuracy can actually get worse.\n",
    "\n",
    "This issue is called the **degradation problem**.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Why Deep Networks Become Difficult\n",
    "\n",
    "Suppose we build:\n",
    "\n",
    "* 10-layer CNN → works well\n",
    "* 50-layer CNN → should work better theoretically\n",
    "\n",
    "But in practice:\n",
    "\n",
    "* gradients vanish/explode\n",
    "* optimization becomes difficult\n",
    "* deeper models may perform worse\n",
    "\n",
    "Even with techniques like:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Batch Normalization\n",
    "* Better initialization\n",
    "* ReLU activation\n",
    "\n",
    "very deep networks were still hard to optimize.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Core Idea of ResNet\n",
    "\n",
    "Instead of learning:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "H(x)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "ResNet learns the **residual**:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "F(x) = H(x) - x\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "So the original function becomes:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "H(x) = F(x) + x\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This is implemented using a **skip connection** (shortcut connection). Where:\n",
    "\n",
    "* $x$ = input\n",
    "* $F(x)$ = output from convolution layers\n",
    "* $H(x)$ = final output\n",
    "\n",
    "The input is directly added to the output of stacked layers.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Architecture of a ResNet\n",
    "\n",
    "Typical ResNet pipeline:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Input Image\n",
    "2. 7×7 Conv\n",
    "3. Max Pool\n",
    "4. Residual Blocks\n",
    "5. Global Average Pooling\n",
    "6. Fully Connected Layer\n",
    "7. Softmax\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why Skip Connections Help\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Without Skip Connection\n",
    "\n",
    "Gradient path:\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "Loss\n",
    " ↑\n",
    "Layer 100\n",
    " ↑\n",
    "Layer 99\n",
    " ↑\n",
    "...\n",
    " ↑\n",
    "Layer 1\n",
    "```\n",
    "Gradients must travel through every layer and Gradients may vanish.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### With Skip Connection\n",
    "\n",
    "Gradient can bypass layers:\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "Loss\n",
    " ↑\n",
    "Add\n",
    " ↑  ↖\n",
    "Conv  Skip\n",
    " ↑\n",
    "Input\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "The skip connection provides a direct path for gradients, helping prevent vanishing gradients and making very deep networks trainable.\n",
    "\n",
    "Benefits:\n",
    "\n",
    "* easier optimization\n",
    "* stable gradients\n",
    "* faster convergence\n",
    "* deeper networks possible\n",
    "\n",
    "### Types of ResNet Architectures\n",
    "\n",
    "Popular variants:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Model     | Layers |\n",
    "| --------- | ------ |\n",
    "| ResNet18  | 18     |\n",
    "| ResNet34  | 34     |\n",
    "| ResNet50  | 50     |\n",
    "| ResNet101 | 101    |\n",
    "| ResNet152 | 152    |\n"
   ]
  },
