    "16 channels\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "Adds 4 more:\n",
    "$$\n",
    "32×32×20\n",
    "$$\n",
    "You can see the gradual growth.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Chracterstics\n",
    "\n",
    "### Advantages\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Efficient Parameter Usage: Learns compact representations.\n",
    "2. Strong Feature Propagation: Information flows easily.\n",
    "3. Better Accuracy  \n",
    "   Works very well for:\n",
    "   * Image classification\n",
    "   * Medical imaging\n",
    "   * Object detection\n",
    "4. Reduced Vanishing Gradient: Deep networks train more reliably.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Disadvantages\n",
    "\n",
    "1. High Memory Usage: Because all feature maps are stored.\n",
    "2. Slower Computation: Concatenation operations increase cost.\n",
    "3. Difficult for Very Large Inputs: Memory can become a bottleneck.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Popular versions\n",
    "\n",
    "| Model        | Layers |\n",
    "| ------------ | ------ |\n",
    "| DenseNet-121 | 121    |\n",
    "| DenseNet-169 | 169    |\n",
    "| DenseNet-201 | 201    |\n",
    "| DenseNet-264 | 264    |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why DenseNet Works Well\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Feature Reuse\n",
    "   Earlier learned features are reused by later layers.  \n",
    "   * Early layer detects edges\n",
    "   * Later layers reuse those edges directly  \n",
    "   * No need to relearn them.\n",
    "2. Better Gradient Flow  \n",
    "   Since all layers are connected:\n",
    "   * Gradients can flow directly backward\n",
    "   * Training very deep networks becomes easier\n",
    "   * This reduces vanishing gradients.\n",
    "3. Fewer Parameters  \n",
    "   DenseNet is surprisingly compact. Because features are reused:\n",
    "   * Fewer filters are needed\n",
    "   * Less redundancy exists  \n",
    "\n",
    "DenseNet often uses fewer parameters than:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Computer Vision models like ResNet\n",
    "* VGG networks\n",
    "\n",
    "while achieving similar or better accuracy.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Comparison: DenseNet vs ResNet\n",
    "\n",
    "| Feature         | DenseNet      | ResNet    |\n",
    "| --------------- | ------------- | --------- |\n",
    "| Connection Type | Concatenation | Addition  |\n",
    "| Feature Reuse   | Strong        | Moderate  |\n",
    "| Parameters      | Fewer         | More      |\n",
    "| Memory Usage    | Higher        | Lower     |\n",
    "| Gradient Flow   | Excellent     | Excellent |\n",
    "\n",
    "# EfficientNet\n",
    "\n",
    "EfficientNet is a family of convolutional neural networks (CNNs) designed to achieve **high accuracy with fewer parameters and lower computational cost** compared to earlier CNN architectures.\n",
    "\n",
    "It was introduced by researchers at Google Research in the 2019 paper:\n",
    "\n",
    "[EfficientNet: Rethinking Model Scaling for Convolutional Neural Networks](https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.11946)\n",
    "\n",
    "The key idea behind EfficientNet is:\n",
    "\n",
    "> Instead of scaling only network depth or width, EfficientNet scales **depth, width, and image resolution together** in a balanced way.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Why EfficientNet Was Needed\n",
    "\n",
    "Before EfficientNet, CNN models were usually improved by increasing one dimension:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Method                   | What Increases | Problem                              |\n",
    "| ------------------------ | -------------- | ------------------------------------ |\n",
    "| Deeper networks          | More layers    | Harder training, diminishing returns |\n",
    "| Wider networks           | More channels  | More memory/computation              |\n",
    "| Higher resolution images | Bigger inputs  | Expensive computation                |\n",
    "\n",
    "Examples:\n",
    "\n",
    "* ResNet became deeper.\n",
    "* Wide ResNet became wider.\n",
    "* Some models only increased image size.\n",
    "\n",
    "The EfficientNet paper showed that **balanced scaling** gives much better efficiency.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Core Idea: Compound Scaling\n",
    "\n",
    "EfficientNet uses a method called **compound scaling**. Instead of arbitrarily increasing dimensions, it scales:\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Depth** → number of layers\n",
    "* **Width** → number of channels\n",
    "* **Resolution** → input image size\n",
    "\n",
    "using fixed scaling coefficients.\n",
    "\n",
    "The scaling principle is:\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\text{depth}=\\alpha^\\phi,\\quad \\text{width}=\\beta^\\phi,\\quad \\text{resolution}=\\gamma^\\phi\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "\n",
    "* $ \\phi $ controls total extra resources\n",
    "* $ \\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma $ are constants found by neural architecture search\n",
    "\n",
    "Constraint:\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\alpha \\cdot \\beta^2 \\cdot \\gamma^2 \\approx 2\n",
    "$$\n",
    "This ensures computation grows in a controlled way.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Think of EfficientNet like upgrading a factory.\n",
    "\n",
    "Instead of:\n",
    "\n",
    "* only hiring more workers (width),\n",
    "* or only adding more floors (depth),\n",
    "\n",
    "EfficientNet improves:\n",
    "\n",
    "* workers,\n",
    "* floors,\n",
    "* workspace size\n",
    "\n",
    "together in balanced proportions. That balance is why it performs so efficiently.\n",
    "\n",
    "## EfficientNet Architecture\n",
    "\n",
    "EfficientNet starts from a baseline model called: `EfficientNet-B0`\n",
    "\n",
    "Then larger versions are created:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Model | Larger By                |\n",
    "| ----- | ------------------------ |\n",
    "| B1    | Slight scaling           |\n",
    "| B2    | More scaling             |\n",
    "| B3    | Bigger                   |\n",
    "| B4    | Bigger                   |\n",
    "| B5    | Larger                   |\n",
    "| B6    | Very large               |\n",
    "| B7    | Largest original version |\n",
    "\n",
    "Later versions:\n",
    "\n",
    "* EfficientNetV2-S\n",
    "* EfficientNetV2-M\n",
    "* EfficientNetV2-L\n",
    "\n",
    "### Building Blocks Used\n",
    "\n",
    "EfficientNet heavily uses:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. MBConv(Mobile Inverted Bottleneck Convolution) Blocks  \n",
    "   * Originally from MobileNetV2  \n",
    "   * Typical pipeline:\n",
    "      1. Input\n",
    "      2. 1x1 Expansion Conv\n",
    "      3. Depthwise Conv\n",
    "      4. Squeeze-and-Excitation\n",
    "      5. 1x1 Projection Conv\n",
    "      6. Output\n",
    "\n",
    "      These blocks reduce computation while keeping accuracy high.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Depthwise Separable Convolution  \n",
    "   * Standard convolution is expensive. EfficientNet uses:\n",
    "      * Depthwise convolution\n",
    "      * Pointwise convolution\n",
    "   to dramatically reduce FLOPs.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Standard Convolution Cost**\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "D_k^2 \\cdot M \\cdot N \\cdot D_f^2\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Depthwise Separable Cost**\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "D_k^2 \\cdot M \\cdot D_f^2 + M \\cdot N \\cdot D_f^2\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This saves huge computation.\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "\n",
    "* $D_k$: kernel size\n",
    "* $M$: input channels\n",
    "* $N$: output channels\n",
    "* $D_f$: feature map size"
